{"conversation_hash": "4f8d37c0b4c6ce06072f02b56c2a7de6", "model": "gpt-3.5-turbo-0301", "timestamp": "2023-04-10 17:54:10+00:00", "conversation": [{"content": "how to make an alias for: git commit -am \"...\" && git push\nwith ... replaced with the git commit message", "country": "The Netherlands", "hashed_ip": "4932acc1966170c4976cdb854f23a44a5c8dcf406fa9ae5c25f4c6b72100b987", "header": {"accept-language": "en-US,en;q=0.5", "user-agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:109.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/111.0"}, "language": "English", "redacted": false, "role": "user", "state": "North Holland", "timestamp": null, "toxic": false, "turn_identifier": 115163}, {"content": "You can create an alias for this command in your shell configuration file (.bashrc or .zshrc):\n\n1. Open your shell configuration file in a text editor: \n\n   ```bash\n   vi ~/.bashrc\n   ```\n\n2. Add the following alias to the file:\n\n   ```bash\n   alias gcp='git commit -am'\n   \n   function gpush() {\n     git push origin \"$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)\"\n   }\n   \n   function gcpush() {\n     gcp \"$1\" && gpush\n   }\n   \n   alias gc='gcpush'\n   ```\n   \n3. Save the file and exit the text editor.\n\n4. Restart your shell or reload the configuration file:\n\n   ```bash\n   source ~/.bashrc\n   ```\n\nNow, you can use the `gc` alias which stands for \"git commit and push\" with the commit message included:\n\n```bash\ngc \"commit message\"\n```\n\nThis will run `git commit -am \"commit message\"` and then `git push origin <current_branch>`.\n\nAlternatively, you can use the `gcp` alias to only commit without pushing:\n\n```bash\ngcp \"commit message\"\n```\n\nAnd run `gpush` to push changes to the remote repository:\n\n```bash\ngpush\n```", "country": null, "hashed_ip": null, "header": null, "language": "English", "redacted": false, "role": "assistant", "state": null, "timestamp": "2023-04-10 17:54:10+00:00", "toxic": false, "turn_identifier": 115163}], "turn": 1, "language": "Engli